Sval: per-site, per-session noise on audio output a page reads

A new RFPTarget, AudioOutputRandomization, scales each sample of the audio
output a page reads by its own gain between 0.999 and 0.9999: an
OfflineAudioContext's rendered buffer, once, before getChannelData or
copyFromChannel can read it; the samples an AnalyserNode's time-domain and
frequency data are computed from; the input a ScriptProcessorNode hands its
audioprocess event; the input and connected param values an
AudioWorkletProcessor's process() reads; and a DynamicsCompressorNode's
reduction. Brave farbles audio with one gain per site in [0.999, 1). No gain
moves a sample by more than 0.01 dB, so nothing audible changes, yet every
float32 sample, and so a render hash, differs between sites.

SvalRFPService::AudioOutputNoiseKey draws a key from the fingerprinting
randomization key canvas noise uses: the key cookieJarSettings carries,
derived from the browsing session key and the top-level site, so it is
fixed for a site within a session, and private browsing has its own.
SvalRFPService::RandomizeAudioOutput draws each gain from that key, the
channel, the sample's index and a hash of the samples, as canvas noise
hashes the image: a base the key alone fixes plus a jitter, so even the
largest gain a page can learn from many renders differs between sites. A
reference signal rendered beside a fingerprint, such as a ConstantSourceNode
on another channel or in another context, draws unrelated gains and cannot
divide them out. The key is made when any randomization target is on:
GetBrowsingSessionKey and GenerateKey check one list of them,
kRandomizationTargets. Each node computes its context's noise key once, when
it is made, so no audio callback or getter pays for the HMAC. The target is
Doc-gated, so a site's -AllTargets granular entry gives it the raw output.

No chrome API can change what Web Audio renders.

